# 📈 Stock Price Prediction Using LSTM

A machine learning project that uses **Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)** neural networks to analyze historical stock market data and predict future stock price trends.

## 📌 Project Overview

Stock prices are influenced by historical trends and patterns that can be difficult to identify using traditional statistical approaches. This project explores the use of deep learning, specifically **LSTM networks**, to learn patterns from historical stock price data and generate predictions.

LSTM is a type of Recurrent Neural Network (RNN) that is particularly useful for working with sequential and time-series data.

The goal of this project is to understand how deep learning can be applied to financial time-series forecasting.

---

## 🎯 Objectives

* Collect and preprocess historical stock price data
* Analyze stock price trends
* Prepare sequential time-series data for model training
* Build an LSTM-based deep learning model
* Train the model using historical stock data
* Predict future stock prices
* Compare predicted values with actual values
* Visualize model performance and prediction results

---

## 🧠 Machine Learning Approach

The project follows a typical time-series prediction workflow:

```text
Historical Stock Data
        ↓
Data Cleaning
        ↓
Feature Selection
        ↓
Data Normalization
        ↓
Create Time-Series Sequences
        ↓
Train / Test Split
        ↓
LSTM Model
        ↓
Model Training
        ↓
Price Prediction
        ↓
Visualization & Evaluation
```

---

## 🔬 Why LSTM?

Traditional neural networks do not handle sequential dependencies particularly well. LSTM networks are designed to retain relevant information from previous time steps, making them useful for time-series problems.

In this project, the LSTM model learns relationships between previous stock prices and subsequent price movements.

> **Note:** Stock prices are affected by many external factors. Model predictions should therefore be considered experimental and educational rather than financial advice.

